            %li
              %p
                %strong Chern-Simons form
          %h2#references References
          %p Original eponymous article:
          %ul
            %li#ChernSimons74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shiing-Shen+Chern"}> Shiing-Shen Chern
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Simons"} James Simons
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Characteristic+forms+and+geometric+invariants"} Characteristic forms and geometric invariants
              The Annals of Mathematics, Second Series,
              %strong 99
              1 (1974) 48-69 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/pss/1971013"}> jstor:1971013
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~hk244/chern-simons.cp.pdf"} pdf
          %p Reviews:
          %ul
            %li#Nakahara03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Nakahara"}> Mikio Nakahara
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chern-Simons forms
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry%2C+Topology+and+Physics"} Geometry, Topology and Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9781315275826"} doi:10.1201/9781315275826
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://alpha.sinp.msu.ru/~panov/LibBooks/GRAV/(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)Mikio_Nakahara-Geometry,_Topology_and_Physics,_Second_Edition_(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)-Institute_of_Physics_Publishing(2003).pdf"} pdf
            %li#Lam15
              %p
                Kai S. Lam:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chern-Simons forms
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topics in Contemporary Mathematical Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789814667814_0042"} doi;10.1142/9789814667814_0042
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Lam-CSForms.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p The statements about “pure shift” paths are reviewed on the first few pages of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Simons"}> James Simons
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Sullivan"} Dennis Sullivan
              %em Structured vector bundles define differential K-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.4935?context=math.DG"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            which discusses the relevance of Chern-Simons forms in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
          %p
            While
            %a{:href => "#ChernSimons74"} Chern &amp; Simons 1974
            was motivated (as stated in the introduction there) by an attempt to find a combinatorial formula for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+class"} Pontrjagin class
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            (i.e. that associated to the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} O(n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            to which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            is associated), the Chern-Simons form appeared as a boundary term that obstructed to original attempt to derive the Pontrjagin class by integrating curvature classes simplex-by-simplex.
          %p But a combinatorial formula of the kind these authors were looking for was however (nevertheless) given later in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Luc+Brylinski"}> Jean-Luc Brylinski
              , Dennis McLaughlin:
              %em Čech cocycles for characteristic classes
              , Comm. Math. Phys.
              %strong 178
              (1996) 225-236 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02104916"}> doi:10.1007/BF02104916
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-formulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} L-∞-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0801.3480"} SSS08
          %p An abstract algebraic model of the algebra of Chern’s characteristic classes and Chern-Simons secondary characteristic classes and of the gauge group action on this algebra (which also enables some noncommutative generalizations) is pioneered in 2 articles
          %ul
            %li
              Israel M. Gelfand, Mikhail M. Smirnov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The algebra of Chern-Simons classes, the Poisson bracket on it, and the action of the gauge group
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 123
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chern-Simons classes and cocycles on the Lie algebra of the gauge group
          %p On the geometric interpretation of the Chern-Simons invariant for more general principal bundles:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+%C4%8Cap"}> Andreas Čap
              , Keegan J. Flood, Thomas Mettler:
              %em Flat extensions and the Chern-Simons 3-form
              (2024) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12811"}> arXiv:2409.12811
              \&amp;rbrack;
